Zestix / Sentap Cloud-Sharing Credential Campaign

InfinityGrid ID: IG-2026-004
Status: Active / Corroborated
Sector: Cross-sector
Region: Global
Threat Actor: Zestix / Sentap
Incident Type: Credential compromise / cloud data theft
Primary Platforms: ShareFile, ownCloud, Nextcloud
Initial Public Reporting: January 2026
Priority: High
Last Reviewed: August 2026

Summary

A global cybercrime campaign attributed by researchers to the threat actor Zestix, also known as Sentap, targeted corporate cloud file-sharing environments using valid credentials obtained from information-stealing malware infections.

The campaign is notable because publicly available evidence does not point to a newly exploited software vulnerability as the primary access path. Instead, researchers found that credentials stolen from infected employee systems remained valid and were used to authenticate directly to corporate file-sharing platforms.

Affected environments reportedly included ShareFile, ownCloud, and Nextcloud instances associated with dozens of organizations across multiple industries.

What Happened

Hudson Rock researchers investigated data that Zestix was advertising for sale and linked the material to credentials captured in infostealer logs. The credentials were associated with malware families including RedLine, Lumma, and Vidar.

The attacker reportedly used those credentials to sign into enterprise file-sharing systems and retrieve corporate data.

According to reporting on the investigation, the affected accounts commonly did not enforce multi-factor authentication. This allowed possession of a username and password to remain sufficient for access.

